hi welcome back to our series on  embedding looker  previously we walked you through how to  embed a looker dashboard with custom  filters  now let's dive into another common  embedding use case you can try out  dynamically changing a looker embedded  dashboards layout  let's say you have a looker dashboard  embedded in an iframe in your host  application's front end  the dashboard contains multiple tiles  we want to give the user a more  interactive experience  so let's add a button in your host  application front end that hides a tile  in the embedded dashboard  poof  this dynamically changes the layout of  the embedded dashboard  we will use JavaScript  and our embed SDK to implement the  feature  you can also use plain JavaScript  without the embed SDK 2.  at a high level we will follow these  implementation steps  we will listen for an event emitted by  the iframe that tells us the dashboards  layout then we'll determine the new  dashboard layout AKA which tell it to  hide  lastly we'll set up a new button to  message the iframe to update its layout  we have a working code sample in our  embed reference Repository  links below  keep in mind the code sample is more  complex and makes use of react  we'll walk through a simpler example in  this video  as always a Quick Knowledge check before  we continue  you should be familiar with JavaScript  HTML the looker dashboard and the looker  embed SDK  [Music]  all right let's get started by listening  to the iframe  the embedded dashboard in the iframe  will emit events that communicate  changes in its state  we want to listen to the dashboard  loaded event that fires when the  dashboard and its tiles have loaded but  its queries are not yet running  the event will tell us the layout of the  dashboard  so  how do we listen to this event  we first go through the basic creation  of an embedded dashboard we call the  embed sdks create dashboard with ID  method  then the opento method  and then the build method however this  time we also make a call to the on  method before the build method call so  we can listen to the dashboard loaded  event  we provide the on method with the  event's name  and an event listener function to handle  the event  also after the build method call we need  to chain a call to the connect method to  establish the sdk's communication with  the iframe and enable the event listener  let's determine what tile to hide to  dynamically change the dashboard layout  if we go back to our example we defined  an event listener function to listen to  the dashboard loaded event  the event listener receives the event as  an argument  this event contains an options property  which itself contains the dashboard  layout components property this property  consists of an array of layout  components each representing a tile in  the dashboard we can take this array and  update it to whatever dashboard layout  we want in this example let's update the  dashboard layout component to an array  with the first element removed  this effectively hides the first tile in  the dashboard  now our new dashboard options represent  the new layout we want  lastly let's set up a new button to  message our embedded dashboard to update  its layout  we first create a button in the Dom  next we set up the buttons on click  Handler to call the set options method  on the embedded dashboard object  returned by the embed SDK  we pass into the set options method the  new dashboard options that Define our  new layout the method sends a message to  the embedded dashboard to update its  options note that the embed SDK provides  the set options method as a convenience  to you you can instead call the send  method with the dashboard option set  message and the same options payload 2.  now when the user clicks on the button  the embedded dashboard receives a  message to dynamically update its layout  and there you have it we listened to the  dashboard loaded event emitted by the  iframe so we could understand the  dashboard's layout  then we determined the new dashboard  layout or rather which tile to hide  lastly we set up a new button to message  the iframe to update itself with the new  layout  now your user can dynamically change the  layout of the embedded dashboard from  within your host applications UI  we hope this use case gives you a  glimpse of the flexibility of looker  embedding we provide open source tools  like the looker embed SDK to help you  embed looker more easily  don't forget to try out the working  sample code in our looker embed  reference Repository  by the way everything I've mentioned  it's linked below and with that  look out for more videos in the series  see you next time  [Music]
 

